<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–7th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Donatists</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donatism was a Christian schism in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> that began in 311 over the election of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Its followers, named after Donatus, held that clergy who had handed over scriptures during the persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> had lost their authority. It became the majority church in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and, despite imperial repression and the arguments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>, lasted for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donatism.jpg" alt="Donatism" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles André van Loo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustine_and_donatists.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
